---
/**
 * The design pass's reference page.
 *
 * It is still placeholder copy — the editorial pass owns every word — but it is now a
 * working exercise of the whole system: the three-track canvas, a margin note attached
 * to a paragraph, a rail label on each section, a breakout table, and both charts in
 * both of their renderings. If something is broken at 390px or at 1440px it is broken
 * here first.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import StackingCeiling from "~/components/charts/StackingCeiling.astro";
import TimeToKnow from "~/components/charts/TimeToKnow.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Rung from "~/components/Rung.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Do the certain things first">
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>The answer</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Do the certain things first</h1>

    <MarginNote label="Where this comes from">
      <p>
        Fund filings and tax statute, not a backtest. The largest single line is the capital gain an active mutual
        fund hands you in December and an exchange-traded fund does not.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>
      Three parts of a portfolio pay you whatever the market does. Keep the fee low, put each holding in the account
      that taxes it least, and stop paying a fund manager to hand you a capital gain in December. That work is worth
      roughly 109 basis points a year against the portfolio a typical saver already holds, and you would know inside
      a year whether you got it.
    </p>

    <p>Everything after that is a wager. This site tells you which is which.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout class="mt-10">
    <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Figure id="structural-budget" />
      <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" />
      <Figure id="effective-bets" />
    </div>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Why</Rail>
    <h2>Stacking works, and it stops working sooner than anyone expects</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Caveat">
      <p>
        The curve is an upper bound. It treats the edge as known. A real edge is estimated, and that error never
        washes out with time.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>
      Pile up enough bets that are each 55% likely to beat the market, the argument goes, and you end up close to
      certain. The arithmetic agrees, and then it asks what the bets have in common. At the correlation this
      portfolio's own sleeves actually show, an unlimited stack of them reaches 57.6% and stops.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Breakout>
    <StackingCeiling />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <Rail>How sure</Rail>
    <h2>How sure we are, in four words</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Why not colour">
      <p>
        A green badge would say two things at once: how sure we are, and whether the news is good. Those are
        different axes, and merging them is the mistake this vocabulary exists to avoid.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>
      Every claim on this site carries one of four rungs. The mark beside the word is drawn in ink alone, at four
      intensities, so it survives a printout and a colour-blind reader. Nothing here is ever green for good news.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-3">
    <Rung level="settled" showGloss />
    <Rung level="probably" showGloss />
    <Rung level="might" showGloss />
    <Rung level="cant-tell" showGloss />
  </div>

  <Callout kind="do-this">
    <p>
      Open your plan statement and read one number: the expense ratio on the fund holding most of your money. Under
      10 basis points, skip to the next section. Over 50, you have found the largest reliable gain available to you
      today.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The horizon</Rail>
    <h2>The difference between the two is how long you would wait to find out</h2>

    <MarginNote label="The arithmetic">
      <p>
        Time to know goes as the <em>square</em> of noise over edge. Forty times the noise is sixteen hundred times
        the wait.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>
      Both of the claims below are worth about a percentage point a year on paper. One of them settles over a
      summer. The other one settles after you are dead. The whole reason to do the boring things first is sitting in
      that gap, and it is not a difference in size.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Breakout>
    <TimeToKnow />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <Rail>The evidence</Rail>
    <h2>Where the money actually comes from</h2>

    <MarginNote>
      <p>
        These three never add up. A saving measured against your own current holdings says nothing about whether you
        beat an index fund.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>
      The three benchmarks below answer different questions, and their results may never be added together. The code
      that assembles the edge budget raises an error rather than summing across them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[32rem] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">Compared with</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4 text-right">Edge</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4 text-right">Spread</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2">How sure</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-ink-muted">
        <tr class="border-b border-rule">
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">The portfolio you hold now</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">109 bp/yr</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">41 bp</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="settled" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule">
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">A cheap total-market index</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">5.4 bp/yr</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">313 bp</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="cant-tell" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">The average investor's behaviour</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">15 bp/yr</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">150 bp</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="might" /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <Callout kind="change-your-mind">
    <p>
      A fund shelf that cut its published edge below 20 basis points a year, or a decade of live returns in which the
      tilted sleeves trailed the index by more than their stated spread, would move the second row from a coin flip
      to a rejection.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Base>
